entsearch -- search .map sources for entities
=============================================

What it is for
--------------
Mapping runs on precedent. You want to know what value someone else put in a
property, which classnames actually use it, and whether the combination you
are about to try has been done before. That answer lives in .map sources --
Raven's, the mission pack's, whatever you have decompiled -- and it is spread
across thousands of entities in dozens of files, which is exactly the shape of
question a text editor's find box is bad at.

entsearch takes three regular expressions -- a classname, a property name and
a value -- and prints every entity in every map that matches all three, with
the file, the line, the coordinates, and flag values broken out into the boxes
an editor would draw.


Credit
------
The design is not ours. Inky's MapSearch does this, and it is where every idea
here comes from: the three-argument search, matching an integer against the
bits of spawnflags, the .pts point files, the shape of the config.

    http://earthday.free.fr/Inkys-Hexen-II-Mapping-Corner/

His is the reference implementation. If you are on Windows, use it -- it is
his tool, he maintains it, and it is the one that will keep getting his
attention. entsearch exists because the uHexen2 toolchain wants an entity
search that builds from source next to qbsp and light on every platform we
ship, and it shares no code with his.


Command line
------------
    entsearch CLASSNAME [PROPERTY] [VALUE] [OPTIONS]

Only CLASSNAME is required. The more of the three you give, the narrower the
search. All three are regular expressions.

Leave one out, or pass "*" or ".*", to accept anything.

  IMPORTANT on a Unix shell: quote the "*", or the shell expands it to the
  names of the files in the current directory before entsearch sees it.
  Write 'entsearch "*" spawnflags 64', not 'entsearch * spawnflags 64'.

A pattern has to match the WHOLE name or value, not a part of it. So
"func_door" does not find func_door_rotating; "func_door.*" does. Likewise a
sound reference is ".*\.wav", not "\.wav".

Supported syntax:

    .              any character
    [abc] [^a-z]   character classes, with ranges and negation
    \d \D          a digit / anything else
    \w \W          a word character ([A-Za-z0-9_]) / anything else
    \s \S          whitespace / anything else
    \. \\ \* ...   any punctuation character, taken literally
    \t \n \r       tab, newline, carriage return
    (...)          grouping; (?:...) is accepted and means the same
    a|b            alternation
    ^ $            start and end of the value
    * + ?          zero or more, one or more, zero or one
    {n} {n,} {n,m} counted repetition
    *? +? ?? {}?   the lazy forms of all of the above

Case is ignored by default. Turn that off with the "i" option or in the config
file, and "light_torch_castle" stops finding an entity spelled
Light_Torch_Castle.

When VALUE is an integer it is matched two ways: literally, and as a set of
checked boxes inside the flag properties (see FlagsProperties below). So

    entsearch "*" spawnflags 2

finds every entity with AT LEAST the 2 box checked -- in the siege maps that
turns up values 2, 3, 34, 35, 66, 67, 98 and 131, because none of the other
boxes are being asked about. There is no direct way to ask for exactly one
combination and nothing else; either post-filter the output, or take
spawnflags out of the FlagsProperties list in the config, which turns it back
into a plain integer that only matches exactly.


[OPTIONS]
---------
A run of the letters below, each switched on by a preceding "+" (assumed if
you write neither) or off by a "-". A letter you leave out keeps whatever the
config file says.

    l   also write the results to a log file
    n   sort by line number, instead of classname then targetname then
        property name
    p   write a .pts point file for each map with results, named after the
        map and beside it. Open it in TrenchBroom to walk from one hit to
        the next.
    i   ignore case (on unless the config says otherwise)
    d   developer mode: report on stderr what is being read and skipped

    +l-p    write a log, no point files, default sort order
    -p+l    the same
    l-p     the same again, since "+" is assumed
    lnp     switch on l, n and p

A bare run of letters like "lnp" is only read as options once CLASSNAME,
PROPERTY and VALUE have all been given; before that it would be one of them.
Anything starting with "+" or "-" is read as options wherever it appears --
except a negative number, so searching for the value -1 works.

Three arguments are not options:

    --config=FILE   read this config file instead of looking for one
    --help          the built-in usage summary
    --version       version information

Exit status is 0 if anything matched, 1 if nothing did, and 2 on an error, so
this drops into a script or a shell loop without parsing the output.


entsearch-config.xml
--------------------
The settings you do not want to retype on every run. entsearch looks for it,
in order, at:

    the path given by --config=FILE
    the path in the ENTSEARCH_CONFIG environment variable
    ./entsearch-config.xml
    entsearch-config.xml next to the entsearch binary

Without one, "." is searched recursively with the built-in flag property list
and every output option off. A commented sample sits next to this README. It
holds four things:

1) SearchIn -- the directories to walk, each one recursively. "." is the
   current directory. Naming a single .map file instead of a directory also
   works.

2) Exclude -- paths to skip, each a regular expression that has to match the
   whole thing. Every entry is tried three ways: against the full path with
   "/" separators, against the full path with "\" separators, and against the
   bare file or directory name. Skipping a directory skips everything below
   it. Trying the bare name is what lets a plain "autosave" drop
   TrenchBroom's autosave folders wherever they are, on either platform,
   without a pattern that only works on one of them.

3) FlagsProperties -- the properties whose value is a set of check boxes
   rather than a number, spawnflags being the obvious one. These are what an
   integer VALUE is bit-matched against, and their values are printed with
   the bits spelled out. Listing the section replaces the built-in list
   rather than adding to it, which is how you take a property out.

   The built-in list is spawnflags plus the fields the Hexen II HexenC source
   actually combines with the (+) bitwise-or operator: flags, flags2,
   effects, items, items2, drawflags and artifact_flags. Change it for
   another game.

4) The output options -- the same set as [OPTIONS] above, plus the log file
   name and a directory to put .pts files in if you would rather not write
   them next to the maps.  A letter on the command line beats what is here.

The parser is loose on purpose. Element names are matched without regard to
case, the root element is ignored, and a value may be written any of four
ways:

    <SearchIn>path</SearchIn>            text in the section, one per line
    <SearchIn><Path>p</Path></SearchIn>  a leaf holding text; its name ignored
    <FlagsProperties><spawnflags/>       a leaf whose NAME is the value, with
                                         exclude="true" to park it
    <Output MakePts="true" />            settings written as attributes

The last two are how the sample config Inky ships writes its flag properties
and its output settings. So if you already keep a config for MapSearch,
copying it to entsearch-config.xml is the whole migration -- that is tested,
not assumed: his sample config loads here and produces the same results his
tool does.


A note about origins
--------------------
Every match is printed with the x y z of its entity, so you can paste it into
TrenchBroom's Menu > View > Camera > Move Camera to... box.

For a point entity those are the coordinates in its "origin" property. A brush
entity has no origin property, so the coordinates are the first point of the
first plane of its first brush, and such a line is marked [brush]. A .map
brush is stored as a set of planes, each given by three points, and nothing
requires those points to sit on the finished brush -- they only have to define
the plane. So the coordinate is a neighbourhood, not a position, and it can
land well outside the brush it belongs to. It is close enough to be useful far
more often than not, which is the only defence it needs.


Examples
--------
    entsearch "*" spawnflags 64
Every entity, whatever its classname, with the 64 box checked in spawnflags.
65 and 192 match too: the other boxes are not asked about.

    entsearch "*" "*" ".*\.wav"
Every property of every entity that names a wav file -- the fastest way to
find out which sounds a map actually references.

    entsearch light_torch.* light "*"
Every torch variant and the light value it was given. Swapping in "*" for the
classname is how you find out whether anything else in the map carries a
light key, which is not always what you expect.

    entsearch "*" spawnflags 2
Bit matching in practice. Run against the siege maps in gamecode/hc-unused,
this returns 33 results across values 2, 3, 34, 35, 66, 67, 98 and 131 --
every entity with that box checked, whatever else is checked with it:

    Line  15132:  (trigger_teleport)     spawnflags          == 131 [1][2][128]   at  -768 -1872 176  [brush]
    Line  21642:  (func_door_rotating)   opendraw.spawnflags == 98 [2][32][64]    at  -112 -264 -12   [brush]
    Line  19810:  (art_cubeofforce)      spawnflags          == 2 [2]             at  62 418 723

    entsearch func_door.* classname
Every func_door and func_door_rotating, one line each. "classname" is the one
property that always exists, so naming it is how you ask for one line per
entity rather than one line per property.

    entsearch func_door_rotating
Name no property at all and you get every property of every match, which is
the quickest way to read an unfamiliar entity's full setup.

    entsearch "*" "*" 128 +p
Anything set to 128 anywhere, plus a .pts file per map so you can step through
the hits in TrenchBroom. Flag properties and plain numbers both answer: a
spawnflags of 160 matches on its 128 bit and prints [32][128], while a lip of
128 matches only because it is exactly 128.
